# Spec layers and kit boundaries

## Vocabulary (agent engineering)

When discussing **agent** or **LLM product** work, prefer explicit layers so `/spec`, `/plan`, and runtime design stay aligned:

| Layer | Meaning (short) | Typical owner in a consumer repo |
|-------|-----------------|----------------------------------|
| **Contracts** | Tool schemas, structured I/O, API shapes | Code + schema artifacts |
| **Workflow** | Graphs, steps, orchestration, checkpoints | Application / orchestration code |
| **Policy** | Guardrails, middleware, allow/deny, PII rules | Middleware, config, policy services |
| **Test / eval** | Datasets, rubrics, graders, regression suites | Tests, eval platforms, CI |
| **Ops / observability** | Traces, metrics, audit logs, alerting | Telemetry stack, dashboards |

Kit specs under `specs/` usually emphasize **product intent, acceptance criteria, and non-goals**. They may **mention** which layers are in scope for a feature; they do not replace executable contracts or eval code.

## What agent-sdlc is / is not

- **Is:** Markdown-and-config **SDLC** kit (Cursor rules, slash commands, Claude mirror, templates, skills pointers).
- **Is not:** Application runtime, package manifests, LangGraph/LangSmith/OpenAI SDK code, or CI eval runners.

For handoff language when work leaves the editor kit, see the **agent-runtime-handoff** skill under `.cursor/skills/`.

